(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Explain Fubini's Theorem for double integrals over rectangular regions. Evaluate the double integral: I = iint_R \(x^2 + y^2\) dA over the rectangle R = [0, 2] x [0, 3] in both orders of integration.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Formal Statement of Fubini's Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   Let f\(x, y\) be a continuous real-valued function on a closed bounded rectangle R = [a, b] x [c,) Tj
T*
(   d].) Tj
T*
(   Then the double integral of f over R exists and can be evaluated by iterated integrals in) Tj
T*
(   either order:) Tj
T*
(   iint_R f\(x, y\) dA = int_a^b [ int_c^d f\(x, y\) dy ] dx = int_c^d [ int_a^b f\(x, y\) dx ] dy.) Tj
T*
(   2. Evaluation Order 1 \(Integrate with respect to y first, then x\):) Tj
T*
(   I = int_0^2 [ int_0^3 \(x^2 + y^2\) dy ] dx.) Tj
T*
(   - Inner integral:) Tj
T*
(   int_0^3 \(x^2 + y^2\) dy = [ x^2 * y + y^3 / 3 ]_0^3 = [ 3*x^2 + 27 / 3 ] - 0 = 3*x^2 + 9.) Tj
T*
(   - Outer integral:) Tj
T*
(   I = int_0^2 \(3*x^2 + 9\) dx = [ x^3 + 9*x ]_0^2 = [ 2^3 + 9\(2\) ] - 0 = 8 + 18 = 26.) Tj
T*
(   3. Evaluation Order 2 \(Integrate with respect to x first, then y\):) Tj
T*
(   I = int_0^3 [ int_0^2 \(x^2 + y^2\) dx ] dy.) Tj
T*
(   - Inner integral:) Tj
T*
(   int_0^2 \(x^2 + y^2\) dx = [ x^3 / 3 + y^2 * x ]_0^2 = [ 8/3 + 2*y^2 ] - 0 = 8/3 + 2*y^2.) Tj
T*
(   - Outer integral:) Tj
T*
(   I = int_0^3 \(8/3 + 2*y^2\) dy = [ \(8/3\)*y + 2*y^3 / 3 ]_0^3) Tj
T*
(   = [ \(8/3\)*3 + 2\(27\)/3 ] - 0 = 8 + 18 = 26.) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   Both orders of integration produce identically 26, verifying Fubini's theorem.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Describe the methodology for changing the order of integration in a double integral. Change the order of integration and evaluate: I = int_0^a int_{x^2/a}^{2a - x} x * y dy dx.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Methodology for Changing Order of Integration:) Tj
T*
(   - Identify the original bounding curves from the limits:) Tj
T*
(   Inner limits: y varies from y = x^2 / a \(parabola\) to y = 2a - x \(straight line\).) Tj
T*
(   Outer limits: x varies from x = 0 to x = a.) Tj
T*
(   - Find intersection of bounding curves:) Tj
T*
(   x^2 / a = 2a - x => x^2 + ax - 2a^2 = 0 => \(x + 2a\)\(x - a\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   In the first quadrant, x = a. At x = a: y = a^2 / a = a. Intersection point P\(a, a\).) Tj
T*
(   At x = 0: lower curve y = 0, upper curve y = 2a.) Tj
T*
(   - Partition region R for horizontal slicing \(dy first, then dx\):) Tj
T*
(   As y varies from 0 to 2a, the boundary changes nature at y = a:) Tj
T*
(   * Sub-region R1 \(0 <= y <= a\):) Tj
T*
(   x varies from x = 0 \(y-axis\) to the parabola x = sqrt\(a * y\).) Tj
T*
(   * Sub-region R2 \(a <= y <= 2a\):) Tj
T*
(   x varies from x = 0 to the straight line x = 2a - y.) Tj
T*
(   2. New Form of Integral:) Tj
T*
(   I = int_0^a [ int_0^sqrt\(ay\) x*y dx ] dy + int_a^\(2a\) [ int_0^\(2a - y\) x*y dx ] dy = I1 + I2.) Tj
T*
(   3. Evaluate I1:) Tj
T*
(   Inner integral: int_0^sqrt\(ay\) x*y dx = y * [ x^2 / 2 ]_0^sqrt\(ay\) = y * \(ay / 2\) = \(a / 2\) *) Tj
T*
(   y^2.) Tj
T*
(   Outer integral: I1 = int_0^a \(a / 2\) * y^2 dy = \(a / 2\) * [ y^3 / 3 ]_0^a = a^4 / 6.) Tj
T*
(   4. Evaluate I2:) Tj
T*
ET

