(>> Solution to Question 20:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State Stokes' Theorem for a vector field F. Give its physical interpretation and state how it reduces to Green's Theorem in the xy-plane.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Formal Statement of Stokes' Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   Let S be an oriented piecewise smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise smooth) Tj
T*
(   boundary curve C with positive orientation matching S \(right-hand rule\).) Tj
T*
(   If F is a continuous vector field with continuous first partial derivatives in a domain) Tj
T*
(   containing S, then:) Tj
T*
(   oint_C F . dr = iint_S \(curl F\) . n dS,) Tj
T*
(   where n is the outward unit normal vector to surface S.) Tj
T*
(   2. Physical Interpretation:) Tj
T*
(   - Left-hand side oint_C F . dr: represents the macroscopic Circulation \(work done / fluid) Tj
T*
(   circulation\) of vector field F along the boundary loop C.) Tj
T*
(   - Right-hand side iint_S \(curl F\) . n dS: represents the net flux of the microscopic Vorticity) Tj
T*
(   \(curl F\) passing through surface S.) Tj
T*
(   - Physical meaning: The total macroscopic fluid circulation around boundary loop C is exactly) Tj
T*
(   equal to the sum of all microscopic whirlpool rotations \(vorticities\) enclosed by the boundary.) Tj
T*
(   3. Reduction to Green's Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   - Let surface S lie entirely in the xy-plane \(z = 0\), so the unit normal is n = k.) Tj
T*
(   - Let F = P\(x, y\)*i + Q\(x, y\)*j + 0*k.) Tj
T*
(   - Compute curl\(F\):) Tj
T*
(   curl\(F\) = [ dQ/dx - dP/dy ] * k.) Tj
T*
(   - The dot product with n = k is:) Tj
T*
(   \(curl F\) . n = \(curl F\) . k = dQ/dx - dP/dy.) Tj
T*
(   - Since dS = dA:) Tj
T*
(   iint_S \(curl F\) . n dS = iint_R [ dQ/dx - dP/dy ] dA.) Tj
T*
(   - Since dr = dx*i + dy*j:) Tj
T*
(   oint_C F . dr = oint_C \(P dx + Q dy\).) Tj
T*
(   - Equating both sides reproduces Green's Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   oint_C \(P dx + Q dy\) = iint_R [ dQ/dx - dP/dy ] dA. \(Proved\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 21:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Verify Stokes' Theorem for F = \(2x - y\)*i - y*z^2*j - y^2*z*k over the upper hemisphere surface x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1 \(z >= 0\), bounded by the circle x^2 + y^2 = 1 in the plane z = 0.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Compute curl\(F\):) Tj
T*
(   | i j k |) Tj
T*
(   | d/dx d/dy d/dz |) Tj
T*
(   | 2x-y -yz^2 -y^2*z |) Tj
T*
(   - i-comp: d/dy\(-y^2*z\) - d/dz\(-yz^2\) = -2yz - \(-2yz\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - j-comp: d/dz\(2x-y\) - d/dx\(-y^2*z\) = 0 - 0 = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - k-comp: d/dx\(-yz^2\) - d/dy\(2x-y\) = 0 - \(-1\) = 1.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore: curl\(F\) = 0*i + 0*j + 1*k = k.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Evaluate the Surface Integral iint_S \(curl F\) . n dS:) Tj
T*
(   - By Stokes' Theorem, the surface integral depends only on the boundary curve C!) Tj
T*
(   Instead of integrating over the hemisphere, we can choose the flat circular disk S1: x^2 + y^2) Tj
T*
(   <= 1 in the plane z = 0 with normal n = k.) Tj
T*
ET

