(>> Solution to Question 26:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Evaluate the line integral oint_C \(z dx + x dy + y dz\) along the intersection of the sphere x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = a^2 and the plane x + y + z = 0 using Stokes' Theorem.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Geometry of the Boundary Curve C:) Tj
T*
(   - The curve C is the intersection of a sphere with a plane passing through the origin.) Tj
T*
(   - It is a great circle of radius R = a lying entirely on the plane x + y + z = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Let S be the flat circular disk inside C lying on the plane x + y + z = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Area of S = pi * a^2.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: Normal Vector to Surface S:) Tj
T*
(   - Equation of plane: x + y + z = 0.) Tj
T*
(   - Normal vector: N = i + j + k.) Tj
T*
(   - Unit normal vector: n = \(i + j + k\) / sqrt\(1^2 + 1^2 + 1^2\) = \(i + j + k\) / sqrt\(3\).) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Compute curl\(F\) where F = z*i + x*j + y*k:) Tj
T*
(   | i j k |) Tj
T*
(   | d/dx d/dy d/dz |) Tj
T*
(   | z x y |) Tj
T*
(   - i-comp: d/dy\(y\) - d/dz\(x\) = 1 - 0 = 1.) Tj
T*
(   - j-comp: d/dz\(z\) - d/dx\(y\) = 1 - 0 = 1.) Tj
T*
(   - k-comp: d/dx\(x\) - d/dy\(z\) = 1 - 0 = 1.) Tj
T*
(   Therefore: curl\(F\) = i + j + k.) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Compute Dot Product \(curl F\) . n:) Tj
T*
(   \(curl F\) . n = \(i + j + k\) . [ \(i + j + k\) / sqrt\(3\) ]) Tj
T*
(   = \(1 + 1 + 1\) / sqrt\(3\) = 3 / sqrt\(3\) = sqrt\(3\).) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Apply Stokes' Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   oint_C F . dr = iint_S \(curl F\) . n dS) Tj
T*
(   = iint_S sqrt\(3\) dS = sqrt\(3\) * iint_S 1 dS) Tj
T*
(   = sqrt\(3\) * Area\(S\)) Tj
T*
(   = sqrt\(3\) * \(pi * a^2\) = pi * a^2 * sqrt\(3\).) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   The value of the line integral is pi * a^2 * sqrt\(3\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 27:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Prove that the field of flow of an incompressible fluid has zero divergence \(div v = 0\) from the law of conservation of mass. Formulate this via Gauss' Divergence Theorem.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Physical Setup:) Tj
T*
(   - Consider an arbitrary control volume V fixed in space, bounded by a closed surface S with) Tj
T*
(   outward unit normal n.) Tj
T*
(   - Let rho\(x, y, z, t\) be fluid mass density and v\(x, y, z, t\) be the velocity vector field.) Tj
T*
(   2. Mass Balance Equation:) Tj
T*
(   - Total mass of fluid within volume V at time t:) Tj
T*
(   M\(t\) = iiint_V rho dV.) Tj
T*
(   - Time rate of mass accumulation inside V:) Tj
T*
(   dM / dt = d/dt [ iiint_V rho dV ] = iiint_V \(drho / dt\) dV.) Tj
T*
(   - Mass flux of fluid escaping across the boundary surface S per unit time:) Tj
T*
(   Mass Outflow = iint_S \(rho * v\) . n dS.) Tj
T*
(   - By the Law of Conservation of Mass \(continuity\), assuming no internal sources or sinks:) Tj
T*
(   Accumulation = - Outflow) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
