(>> Solution to Question 29:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: In an immobilized enzyme spherical bead of radius R, substrate diffuses radially and undergoes zero-order reaction: \(D / r^2\) * d/dr \(r^2 * dC/dr\) = k0. Integrate this second-order ODE in spherical coordinates subject to boundary conditions dC/dr\(0\) = 0 and C\(R\) = Cs, and determine the concentration profile C\(r\).) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   Step 1: Differential Equation:) Tj
T*
(   \(D / r^2\) * d/dr [ r^2 * \(dC / dr\) ] = k0) Tj
T*
(   => d/dr [ r^2 * \(dC / dr\) ] = \(k0 / D\) * r^2.) Tj
T*
(   Step 2: First Integration:) Tj
T*
(   r^2 * \(dC / dr\) = int \(k0 / D\) * r^2 dr = \(k0 / D\) * \(r^3 / 3\) + C1.) Tj
T*
(   Divide by r^2:) Tj
T*
(   dC / dr = \(k0 / \(3*D\)\) * r + C1 / r^2.) Tj
T*
(   Step 3: Apply Boundary Condition at Center \(r = 0\):) Tj
T*
(   For concentration to remain physically finite at the center of the bead \(r = 0\), C1 / r^2 must) Tj
T*
(   not blow up:) Tj
T*
(   C1 = 0 \(also matches symmetry dC/dr\(0\) = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   Therefore:) Tj
T*
(   dC / dr = \(k0 / \(3*D\)\) * r.) Tj
T*
(   Step 4: Second Integration:) Tj
T*
(   C\(r\) = int [ \(k0 / \(3*D\)\) * r ] dr = \(k0 / \(6*D\)\) * r^2 + C2.) Tj
T*
(   Step 5: Apply Boundary Condition at Surface \(r = R\):) Tj
T*
(   C\(R\) = Cs => \(k0 / \(6*D\)\) * R^2 + C2 = Cs) Tj
T*
(   => C2 = Cs - \(k0 * R^2\) / \(6*D\).) Tj
T*
(   Step 6: Assemble Final Concentration Profile:) Tj
T*
(   C\(r\) = Cs - \(k0 * R^2\) / \(6*D\) + \(k0 / \(6*D\)\) * r^2) Tj
T*
(   = Cs - [ k0 / \(6*D\) ] * \(R^2 - r^2\).) Tj
T*
(   Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(   The radial substrate concentration within the biocatalyst bead follows the parabolic profile:) Tj
T*
(   C\(r\) = Cs - [ k0 / \(6D\) ] * \(R^2 - r^2\).) Tj
T*
(   Center concentration is C\(0\) = Cs - k0*R^2 / \(6D\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 30:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Use the Divergence Theorem to show that for a closed cellular surface S bounding cell volume V, the total diffusive flux of a metabolite governed by Fick's law J = -D * grad\(C\) equals the total volumetric reaction rate iiint_V R_rxn dV at steady state.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Physical Setup:) Tj
T*
(   - Let V represent the intracellular volume enclosed by cell membrane S with outward unit normal) Tj
T*
(   n.) Tj
T*
(   - Substrate concentration inside the cell is C\(x, y, z\).) Tj
T*
(   - Diffusive flux vector across the cell is given by Fick's first law:) Tj
T*
(   J = -D * grad\(C\).) Tj
T*
(   - The volumetric metabolic consumption rate per unit volume is R_rxn\(x, y, z\) >= 0.) Tj
T*
(   2. Integral Mass Balance:) Tj
T*
(   - At steady state, the net rate of substrate diffusing into the cell across membrane S must) Tj
T*
(   equal the total rate of substrate consumed by intracellular metabolic reactions within volume) Tj
T*
(   V:) Tj
T*
(   Net Influx = Total Metabolic Consumption) Tj
T*
(   - iint_S J . n dS = iiint_V R_rxn dV.) Tj
T*
(   - The left-hand side represents net influx because outward flux is iint_S J . n dS.) Tj
T*
(   3. Transformation via Gauss' Divergence Theorem:) Tj
T*
(   - Applying Gauss' Divergence Theorem to the vector field J:) Tj
T*
(   iint_S J . n dS = iiint_V div\(J\) dV.) Tj
T*
(   - Substitute J = -D * grad\(C\):) Tj
T*
(   div\(J\) = div\(-D * grad\(C\)\) = -D * div\(grad\(C\)\) = -D * del^2 C.) Tj
T*
(   - Therefore:) Tj
T*
ET

